        {
          "@type": "FAQPage",
          "mainEntity": [
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Which edition of AWS D1.1 is current?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "AWS D1.1/D1.1M:2025 is current, approved in March 2025, comprising Clauses 1 through 11 with eight normative and eleven informative annexes. Inspection is Clause 8, and the ultrasonic acceptance tables carry the 8.2 and 8.3 designators. The dB values quoted here were read from the publicly published D1.1:2000 text, where the identical tables are numbered 6.2 and 6.3."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Is the D1.1 dB method the same as a DAC or TCG technique?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "No. DAC and TCG compensate for distance inside the instrument so a reflector reads the same amplitude at any depth. D1.1 leaves the instrument uncompensated and applies the correction arithmetically as the attenuation factor. That is why the sound path must be measured and recorded for every indication rather than being absorbed into a curve."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Can phased array replace the manual D1.1 technique?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Only as a variation agreed with the Engineer and recorded in the contract records. The clause permits variations in testing procedure, equipment and acceptance standards, covering transducer sizes, frequencies, couplants and techniques. Without that written agreement and a documented equivalence to the reference-level basis, phased array data has no acceptance criterion to be judged against."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "What happens to a weld rejected by ultrasonic testing?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Welds found unacceptable by ultrasonic testing are repaired by the methods the code permits, and repaired areas are retested ultrasonically with the results tabulated on the original report form where available, or on additional forms. The rejected area is first marked directly over its entire length, with depth from the surface and indication rating noted on adjacent base metal."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Does the contract have to state how much testing is done?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Yes. Information furnished to bidders shall clearly identify the extent of nondestructive testing by type, category or location. Full testing means the full length of the joint. Partial testing requires the locations and lengths to be designated. Spot testing requires the number of spots per category in a stated length, and each spot covers at least 4 in. (100 mm) of weld."
              }
            },
            {
              "@type": "Question",
              "name": "Can Atlantis review our D1.1 ultrasonic procedure and reports?",
              "acceptedAnswer": {
                "@type": "Answer",
                "text": "Yes. We write and approve ultrasonic procedures against the clause and table structure described here, run the code-specific practical examination for operators, and audit completed report forms for the arithmetic errors that move indications across class boundaries. Affordable, accessible, fully customizable; ask for a demo or a quote through [contact](/contact)."
              }
            }
          ]
        }
